beat: Beat()
¶@Beat has no value until music plays, so it always needs a guard. This is
the only clock in the program.¶
count: beat•ø ? 0 beat.count

¶One line: the words, the beat it is sung on, how long it holds, and how it
should look. Every start is the first beat of a phrase in the sax that
carries the vocal, and every hold runs out before the next one begins — so
no line holds longer than five beats, which at this tempo is already three
and a half seconds. Some of what the sax plays carries no words at all: an
answering phrase after most halves of a verse (beats 54, 78 and 146), a tail
of long notes after /car/ and after every /burst/, and putting a line on one
of those is what makes the lyric run early. The answering phrase at 169.75
is the one exception, and the melody says so: it is the same six notes with
a pickup in front, which is what a phrase grows when it has a syllable to
land.¶
•Line(words•'' start•# hold•# kind•#)

lines: [
	Line('In the next world war' 39 4 1)
	Line('In a jackknifed juggernaut' 43 5 2)
	Line('I am born again' 48 4 3)
	Line('In the neon sign' 63 4 4)
	Line('Scrolling up and down' 67 5 4)
	Line('I am born again' 72 4 3)
	Line('In an interstellar burst' 87 4.5 5)
	Line('I am back to save the universe' 95 5 1)
	Line('In a deep, deep sleep' 131 4 1)
	Line('of the innocent' 135 5 1)
	Line('I am born again' 140 5 3)
	Line('In a fast German car' 155 4 6)
	Line("I'm amazed that I survived" 163.25 5 1)
	Line('An airbag saved my life' 169.75 5 7)
	Line('In an interstellar burst' 179 4.5 5)
	Line('I am back to save the universe' 187 5 1)
	Line('In an interstellar burst' 251 4.5 5)
	Line('I am back to save the universe' 259 5 1)
	Line('In an interstellar burst' 275 4.5 5)
	Line('I am back to save the universe' 283 5 1)
]

¶The line whose window contains this beat, or ø between them — which is most
of the song, since it is mostly instrumental.¶
showing: lines.find(
	ƒ(l•Line) (count ≥ l.start) & (count < (l.start + l.hold))
)

¶The treatments, one per kind.¶
ƒ say(l•Line)•Phrase
	(l.kind = 2) ?
		Phrase(l.words rotation: 5° entering: Pose(opacity: 0 rotation: 0°))
	(l.kind = 3) ? Phrase(l.words entering: Pose(scale: 2))
	(l.kind = 4) ?
		Phrase(
			l.words
			color: 🌈(50% 136 337°)
			entering: Sequence(
				{
					0%: Pose(offset: Place(0m -2m))
					50%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 2m))
					100%: Pose(offset: Place(0m -2m))
				}
				duration: 3s
			)
		)
	(l.kind = 5) ? Phrase(l.words entering: Sequence(shake() 0.75s count: 5x))
	(l.kind = 6) ?
		Phrase(
			l.words
			entering: Sequence(
				{
					0%: Pose(offset: Place(-10m 0m))
					50%: Pose(offset: Place(1m 0m))
					100%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m))
				}
				duration: 3s
			)
		)
	(l.kind = 7) ?
		¶It fires like the thing it names: from far off and nearly nothing to
		past its own size in a quarter second, then a small rebound as it
		settles. The z offset is what makes it read as coming at you rather
		than just growing — the stage scales by depth, so travel toward the
		viewer inflates it on its own.¶
		Phrase(
			l.words
			1.5m
			entering: Sequence(
				{
					0%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m 6m) scale: 0.1 opacity: 0)
					55%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m -1.5m) scale: 1.3 opacity: 1)
					75%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m 0.5m) scale: 0.92)
					100%: Pose(offset: Place(0m 0m 0m) scale: 1)
				}
				duration: 0.45s
			)
			resting: Sequence(shake() 0.4s)
		)
	(l.kind = 8) ? Phrase(l.words 0.7m)
	Phrase(l.words)

¶Naming a line after its own words makes it its own output, so the stage
knows it left and can play its exiting @Pose. Without the name every line of
the same kind would be the same output, and the words would swap without
ever fading.¶
ƒ sung(l•Line)•Phrase (
	said: say(l)
	((said.name: l.words).exiting: Pose(opacity: 0)).duration: 0.75s
)

¶Four note glyphs, cycled by the beat. @List/length can't promise it isn't
zero, so the remainder may be ø; the 0 is what that ø becomes.¶
glyphs: ['♩' '♪' '♫' '♬']
glyph: glyphs[((count % glyphs.length()) ?? 0) + 1]

¶The stage has no words for most of the song — the long intro, the gaps
between lines, the outro — so instead of going dark it shows the beat. The
glyph is its own name, so each one enters and leaves as it arrives.¶
ƒ notes()•Phrase
	Phrase(
		glyph
		2m
		name: glyph
		color: Color(50% 0 0°)
		entering: Pose(opacity: 0 scale: 0.6)
		exiting: Pose(opacity: 0)
		duration: 0.3s
	)

¶A @Stage's content can't hold ø, so where there are no words the stage
shows the beat rather than nothing.¶
ƒ words()•Phrase
	showing•ø ? notes() sung(showing)

¶The score: every borrowed track, played at the tempo the lyric counts in.
Each note carries its own length and volume, so nothing here needs a beat —
@Music/tempo alone decides how fast it all goes.¶
song: Music(
	[
		guitar1 guitar2 guitar3 guitar4 violin1 guitar5
		guitar6 guitar7 guitar8 guitar9 guitar10 guitar11
		guitar12 flute1 violin2 synth1 bass1 bass2
		drums1 drums2 drums3 drums4 drums5 drums6
		synth2 bell1 bell2 bell3 bell4 bell5
		bell6 bell7 bell8 bell9 bell10 pad1
		pad2 pad3 sax1 sax2 sax3
	]
	tempo: 84beats/min
	scale: Music.chromatic
	name: 'airbag'
)

Stage(
	[
		words()
		song
	]
	face: 'Phudu'
	background: Color(0 0 0°)
	color: Color(100 0 0°)
)
